Date: Fri, 25 Dec 92 10:13:20 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: Re:  POLL: Split the list?

From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@ra.cs.umb.edu>

>    My opinion is, though, that we should completely reorganize our 
> correspondence.  I have some suggestions based on message ranking,
> mail-reader extensions and digital cash schemes that (i.e. suggestions)
> I'd expect to ripen in the next several days (including the time of Boston
> gathering).  I'd be happy to discuss them.

The only problem I have with this idea is the part about "mail-reader
extensions".  I'm afraid that any system that requires changes to the
mail-reading software is not going to succeed.  There is too large a
variety of programs out there.  Most people don't realize just how
varied are the ways in which people receive and read mail.

So, if we did want to experiment with some kind of fancy
message-ranking scheme, I think it should be something that can be done
at the listserver level.

For example, as a simple proposal, suppose we had a two-tier ranking
system.  I won't speculate here on how the rankings are developed; I'd be
happy to hear Sasha's ideas.  But if we did have such rankings, rather
than just send postings out labelled with the rankings, I would again
call for separating them at the list server.  Send them out to two
different mail lists.

This way people with existing, primitive, mail readers can self-select
whether they want all the messages or only the high-rated messages
(maybe some people would even prefer the low-rated messages).  This would
be done simply by choosing whether to subscribe to one or both mailing
lists.

A variation would be to keep the extropians list as now, sending all
postings out, but to also send the high-rated submissions out on a
second list in addition to the extropians list.  People would still
submit only to "extropians@gnu.ai.mit.edu", and their postings would
still be mailed out to extropians subscribers, but the high-rated
postings would also be mailed out to subscribers to "extropians-a".
Extropians who wanted to keep their blood pressure down could just
subscribe to that list.

The hard part in any of these proposals is going to be to come up with a
ranking system that is at all acceptable.  But if that is done, I think
that implementing it at the list server level is going to be necessary
for it to be really useful.

Hal Finney
